Subject: wasix: recheck pending signals before a wait sleeps again

AsyncifyPoller checks the signal queue, and only a check that finds it
empty subscribes the waker. When the check finds signals, they are
popped and the guest handlers run, and the waker is re-subscribed only
afterwards: a signal delivered while the handlers ran is pushed to a
queue whose waker list is empty and wakes nobody. The poller then
returned Pending with that signal still queued, and the wait slept
until an unrelated wake arrived; for a guest blocked with no timeout,
that is forever.

The window is hit by signals arriving back to back: a handler for the
first is still running when the second lands, and the thread then
sleeps in epoll_wait with the wake it needs already queued. Two
cooperating processes that signal each other and then go quiet
deadlock until any later signal drains the queue. Re-check the queue
after the handlers run and schedule another poll when it is not empty,
so the stragglers are processed before the wait can sleep.

diff --git a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
index 66689e8..90bdb31 100644
--- a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
+++ b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/mod.rs
@@ -423,6 +423,13 @@ where
                         if let Err(err) = WasiEnv::do_pending_link_operations(self.ctx, false) {
                             return Poll::Ready(Err(err));
                         }
+                        // A signal that arrived while the handlers above ran was
+                        // pushed to a queue with no subscribed waker and woke
+                        // nobody; sleeping now would strand it until an
+                        // unrelated wake. Schedule another poll to process it.
+                        if !self.ctx.data().thread.signals().lock().unwrap().0.is_empty() {
+                            cx.waker().wake_by_ref();
+                        }
                         Poll::Pending
                     }
                 }
